from author import *

monopoly_variants={
'P62G-MON-L-024':('Timberline Software faces unchanged downward-sloping demand and rising marginal cost. A production innovation lowers marginal cost by $6 at every output. Assuming an interior profit maximum, how will its output and price change?', ['Both remain unchanged because demand is unchanged.','Output falls and price rises.','Output rises and price rises.','Output rises and price falls.'],3,'Lower MC moves MR = MC to a larger quantity; demand then determines a lower price.'),
'P62G-MON-L-027':('Union Transit faces unchanged demand and marginal cost. Its unavoidable fixed license fee rises by $500. The firm continues operating. What happens to its profit-maximizing price and output?', ['Both rise to cover the fee.','Price rises and output falls.','Both remain unchanged, while profit falls by $500.','Output rises until total revenue covers the fee.'],2,'An unavoidable fixed fee does not change MR or MC. The operating optimum is unchanged and profit falls by the fee increase.'),
'P62G-MON-L-030':('Valley Minerals receives a $4 subsidy for each unit it sells. Demand and production costs are unchanged. At an interior optimum, how should it account for the subsidy?', ['Add $4 to marginal cost and reduce output.','Add $4 to marginal revenue and expand output.','Add $4 to fixed cost without changing output.','Raise the customer price by exactly $4.'],1,'The subsidy increases revenue from each additional unit. MR + $4 = MC occurs at a larger output under the usual downward MR and upward MC curves.'),
'P62G-MON-L-033':('Westbridge Media faces P = 60 − Q and MR = 60 − 2Q, with constant MC = $12. A $6 per-unit tax is introduced. What happens to output and the price paid by customers?', ['Output falls from 24 to 21; price rises from $36 to $39.','Output falls from 24 to 18; price rises from $36 to $42.','Output stays at 24; price rises from $36 to $42.','Output rises from 24 to 27; price falls from $36 to $33.'],0,'Initially 60 − 2Q = 12 gives Q = 24 and P = $36. With the tax, effective MC = $18, so Q = 21 and P = $39. The monopolist does not pass through the full $6 tax.'),
'P62G-MON-L-036':('Xenon Medical Patent faces a new $9 per-unit production tax. Demand is unchanged, MR slopes downward, and MC slopes upward. A manager proposes keeping output unchanged and raising price by $9. What is wrong with that plan?', ['A monopolist can never change its price.','The tax changes only fixed cost.','Higher MC makes profit-maximizing output increase.','Price and quantity must stay on demand; the new MR = MC output is lower.'],3,'The monopolist cannot independently choose price and quantity. Higher effective MC reduces the optimal quantity, and the demand curve determines the new price.'),
'P62G-MON-L-039':('Yellowstone Fiber can choose either a $1,000 unavoidable annual license fee or a $5 fee per unit. It operates under either option. Which policy directly changes its MR = MC output condition?', ['Only the annual fee, because it is the larger dollar amount.','Both fees shift marginal cost by their stated amounts.','Only the per-unit fee, because it raises the cost of an additional unit.','Neither fee, because monopoly output ignores cost.'],2,'The annual fee affects fixed cost and profit. The per-unit fee raises effective marginal cost and therefore changes output incentives.'),
'P62G-MON-L-042':('Zenith Waterworks reduces marginal cost by $7 at every output, but its demand curve also shifts inward. Can the direction of its profit-maximizing output change be determined from this information alone?', ['Yes; output must rise because cost fell.','No; the cost reduction and demand decline have opposing output effects.','Yes; output must fall because demand declined.','No; marginal cost never affects monopoly output.'],1,'Lower MC tends to increase output while lower demand and MR tend to reduce it. Their relative sizes are needed to determine the net output change.')}
for id,(q,o,a,f) in monopoly_variants.items(): replace(id,q,o,a,f,'Diversify the faculty-flagged repeated cost-shift family using fixed costs, subsidies, tax incidence, error diagnosis, and competing shifts.')
