replace('P62G-MON-H-016','A single-price monopolist produces 17 units. At that output, MR equals MC, but price is above MC. With no external costs or benefits, would a small increase in output create a gain for society?', ['No; maximizing monopoly profit also maximizes total surplus.','No; a price above MC shows that buyers value the unit too little.','Yes; any increase in production is socially beneficial.','Yes; marginal willingness to pay exceeds marginal cost.'],3,'The demand price measures marginal willingness to pay. When P > MC, a small increase in output has a positive marginal social gain, even though selling it may not increase the monopolist’s profit.','Replace the repeated rule comparison with a welfare inference and remove unused equations.')
replace('P62G-MON-H-018','A manager tells a single-price monopolist to expand until price equals marginal cost. Why would this generally exceed the profit-maximizing quantity when demand slopes downward?', ['Price equals marginal revenue at every output.','At that quantity, marginal revenue is below marginal cost.','Average total cost must be zero at that quantity.','A monopolist maximizes profit by serving every willing buyer.'],1,'Selling more requires lowering price on existing sales, so MR < P. At P = MC, MR < MC; the firm could raise profit by reducing output.','Replace the duplicated comparison with diagnosis of an incorrect production rule.')
replace('P62G-MON-H-020','A regulator wants a monopoly with no externalities to produce the competitive benchmark quantity. Which condition identifies that quantity, and what additional information is needed to know whether the regulated firm covers total cost?', ['MR = MC; only the number of customers.','P = ATC; only the elasticity of supply.','Minimum ATC; only the firm’s fixed cost.','P = MC; average total cost at that output.'],3,'P = MC identifies the efficient quantity without externalities. Comparing that price with ATC reveals whether total revenue covers total cost.','Use the competitive benchmark in a regulation task instead of repeating the same comparison.')
